{
  "findings": [
    {
      "id": "load-store-swap",
      "text": "Starting with a zero Value and no concurrent calls, Load returns nil. Swap(1) returns nil and installs 1. Store(2) then replaces it, Swap(3) returns 2, and Load returns 3. Store(nil), Swap(nil), and Store or Swap with a string each panic before changing the stored int. On a separate zero Value, Store((*int)(nil)) succeeds and Load returns an interface with concrete type *int and a nil pointer. That interface compares unequal to nil.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 124,
        "fragment": "op.typ, op.data = np.typ, SwapPointer(&vp.data, np.data)"
      },
      "question": "OPEN: Should Load's reference to the most recent Store explicitly include successful Swap and CompareAndSwap calls, which can install a value before any Store call?"
    },
    {
      "id": "compare-and-swap-validation",
      "text": "Starting with a zero Value and no concurrent calls, CompareAndSwap(1, 2) returns false and leaves it empty. CompareAndSwap(nil, 2) then returns true and installs 2. With 2 stored, CompareAndSwap(nil, 3) and CompareAndSwap(1, 3) both return false and leave 2 in place. CompareAndSwap(2, 3) then returns true and Load returns 3. CompareAndSwap(3, nil) panics before inspecting the stored value. CompareAndSwap(1, \"x\") panics even on an empty Value because the arguments have different concrete types. CompareAndSwap(nil, \"x\") panics on the initialized int Value before testing whether old matches.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 141,
        "fragment": "if op.typ != nil && np.typ != op.typ {"
      },
      "question": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "first-write-ordering",
      "text": "Starting with a zero Value, if CompareAndSwap(1, 2) reads the nil type before a concurrent Store(1) claims initialization, it returns false and Store leaves 1 installed. If Store claims initialization first and the compare-and-swap reads the in-progress marker, it spins until Store publishes the int type, then can replace 1 with 2 and return true. A Load that reads the marker returns nil immediately, even if Store has already written the data pointer. While the marker is present, other writers that pass argument validation retry with no cancellation path.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 164,
        "fragment": "if typ == unsafe.Pointer(&firstStoreInProgress) {"
      },
      "question": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "uncomparable-values",
      "text": "Starting with a zero Value, CompareAndSwap(nil, []int{1}) succeeds and installs the slice. A subsequent CompareAndSwap(nil, []int{2}) returns false. CompareAndSwap([]int{1}, []int{2}) instead panics when it compares two interfaces with concrete type []int, before changing the stored slice. Store and Swap accept replacement slices of the same concrete type.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 183,
        "fragment": "if i != old {"
      },
      "question": "OPEN: Should the CompareAndSwap contract document the equality panic when old and the stored value have the same uncomparable concrete type?"
    },
    {
      "id": "compare-and-swap-data-identity",
      "text": "Starting with a comparable value installed, CompareAndSwap can read its data pointer and find that the value equals old. If a concurrent Store then installs an equal value with a different data pointer before the final pointer comparison, CompareAndSwap returns false without retrying. If that Store happens before the data-pointer read, the same CompareAndSwap can succeed in the absence of further writes. If the stored value is a pointer A and concurrent writes replace A with B and then restore A after the read but before the final comparison, CompareAndSwap can also succeed.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 186,
        "fragment": "return CompareAndSwapPointer(&vp.data, data, np.data)"
      },
      "question": "OPEN: Does the contract permit CompareAndSwap to return false when a concurrent write changes the data pointer but the stored value remains equal to old throughout?"
    },
    {
      "id": "copy-after-first-use",
      "text": "Starting with a zero Value, copying it before any method call gives two empty Values that can be initialized independently. Calling Load first returns nil and leaves the receiver empty, but a subsequent copy falls under the documented prohibition if Load counts as first use. The preceding sentence specifically names Store, while the broader sentence also covers use through Swap or CompareAndSwap.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 15,
        "fragment": "A Value must not be copied after first use."
      },
      "question": "OPEN: Does first use include a Load that returns nil or a CompareAndSwap that returns false on an empty Value?"
    }
  ]
}
